abstract:
 &lt;div&gt;
 &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; Steganography enables covert communication by embedding hidden information within digital images, creating significant challenges for digital forensics and cybersecurity investigations. Detecting such hidden content, known as steganalysis, has become increasingly difficult due to adaptive embedding techniques such as WOW and S-UNIWARD.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;In this paper, we present a deep learning-based steganalysis framework using two specialized convolutional neural network architectures: SRNet and an SRM-enhanced VGG19 model. The models were trained and evaluated on the BOSSBase 1.01 dataset using the WOW steganographic algorithm at an embedding rate of 0.4 bits per pixel (bpp).&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;Experimental results demonstrate that the SRM-enhanced VGG19 achieved an accuracy of 83%, while SRNet achieved 80% accuracy. Comparative analysis indicates that high-pass residual preprocessing and architecture specialization significantly improve steganographic feature detection in spatial-domain images.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; The proposed framework provides a practical approach for AIassisted multimedia forensics and highlights the relevance of deep learning-based steganalysis for covert communication detection in digital investigations.
&lt;/div&gt;
